window.docContents[14].resolve({"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--PDE-Problems-and-Differential-Operators--Coordinate-derivatives-and-vector-operators--Why-the-Fr___chet-derivative":{"contents":"The Fréchet derivative requires one continuous linear map  giving the\nuniform approximation  as .\nExistence of a derivative along every line is weaker, even in finite\ndimensions: all directional derivatives can vanish at a point where the\nfunction is not continuous. Even linearity and continuity of the resulting\nmap of directions do not replace the uniform remainder estimate.\nContinuous partial derivatives on a neighborhood are a sufficient\nfinite-dimensional condition for Fréchet differentiability. Using the\nFréchet derivative gives the same chain-rule framework for Euclidean fields\nand for maps between the function spaces used in PDE existence theory.\n\nHere  means that the norm of the error, divided by ,\ntends to zero in every direction at once. For the running polynomial\n, the calculation is exact:\n\n\n\nThe linear part is . Since\n, the error divided by\n is at most , which tends to zero.\nEvaluating  on the two basis vectors gives  and ;\nthese are the entries of the gradient. Differentiating them once more gives\nthe Laplacian .\n\nThe cost of this uniformity is that many natural PDE objects (solutions with\ncorners, shock profiles, and distributional fields) are not Fréchet\ndifferentiable. When that happens, the derivative is replaced by a\ndistributional or weak surrogate from the distribution theory or the Sobolev\ntheory. Regularity theorems then supply conditions under which these weak\nderivatives agree with classical derivatives.\n\nEvans (2010, Appendices A.3–A.4) fixes the finite-dimensional notation;\nthe infinite-dimensional theory originates with Fréchet (1906) and is treated\nsystematically in Cartan (1971) and Lang (1995).\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009PDE Problems and Differential Operators\u0009Coordinate derivatives and vector operators","header":"3.2.2.1. Why the Fréchet derivative","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--PDE-Problems-and-Differential-Operators--Coordinate-derivatives-and-vector-operators--Why-the-Fr___chet-derivative"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--H___lder-Spaces--Interface-with-Morrey-Embedding":{"contents":"The bounded Morrey embedding operator lands in C0GammaOn; composing with\nthe observation maps gives pointwise bounds, while restrictCLM transfers a\nglobal representative to a domain. A separate almost-everywhere theorem\nrelates the selected continuous representative to the original Sobolev base\ncoordinate.\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Extension, Trace, and Embeddings\u0009Hölder Spaces","header":"4.3.3.4. Interface with Morrey Embedding","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--H___lder-Spaces--Interface-with-Morrey-Embedding"}});